      "message": "\u003e So IIUC, this effectively wastes 4 bytes. Is that right ?\n\nYes it does.\n\n\u003e And what if I need to add a struct that\u0027s bigger than 8 bytes ?\n\nThen we are in trouble and have to pack things in \"by hand\" :)\n\n\u003e Is this flexible enough ? (genuine question, I don\u0027t know if by RHEL\u0027s experience we could need more flexibility)\n\u003e \n\nBased on what SUSE and RHEL have done in the past, yes, I think this is all we need.\nAnd RHEL tries to keep the ABI for years, vs. SUSE which just does it for a single year and seems to do just fine with 64bits.\n\n\u003e I\u0027m thinking perhaps we want something like:\n\u003e \n\u003e union {\n\u003e         char android_kabi_reserve[32];\n\u003e         struct {\n\u003e                 unsigned int foo;\n\u003e                 u32 bar;\n\u003e                 struct mystruct baz;\n\u003e                 /* add more here */\n\u003e         };\n\u003e };\n\u003e \n\u003e But I\u0027m not sure what the standard says about unions of different sizes. Would that work ?\n\nIt would, but would be harder to code/verify.  Simplicity is probably best here.",
